WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.940
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.287
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration.

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:09.725
This is Chapter 6: moving
Group Policy as code.

4
00:00:09.725 --> 00:00:13.658
Group Policy analytics tells you
which settings Intune can take,

5
00:00:13.658 --> 00:00:18.100
but not which ones should move, who owns
them, or what's already in the tenant.

6
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.975
Those are the pipeline's questions.

7
00:00:19.975 --> 00:00:25.329
We'll start from Microsoft's own settings
catalog samples, see what they leave out,

8
00:00:25.329 --> 00:00:27.646
then walk the move plan, the library,

9
00:00:27.646 --> 00:00:30.762
the linter and the sync,
two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:30.762 --> 00:00:36.280
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:41.592
Why does this need code at all?

12
00:00:41.592 --> 00:00:44.406
Because the operator
side is already written,

13
00:00:44.406 --> 00:00:47.663
in Book 5's sunset program
and the Intune track's gates,

14
00:00:47.663 --> 00:00:51.069
and what's missing is the
software for two of those steps:

15
00:00:51.069 --> 00:00:54.918
importing the policies for
analysis, and building the library.

16
00:00:54.918 --> 00:00:58.293
Two rules from those pages
shape everything that follows.

17
00:00:58.293 --> 00:01:02.568
Every setting has exactly one
owning policy and one owning tool,

18
00:01:02.568 --> 00:01:05.392
so the library only
holds what Intune owns.

19
00:01:05.392 --> 00:01:08.112
And policies are built
with no assignments;

20
00:01:08.112 --> 00:01:10.831
assigning them is a
separate, reviewed step.

21
00:01:10.831 --> 00:01:14.070
Let's see what Microsoft's
tools say about the stakes.

22
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.734
Here's what Microsoft itself
says about these tools,

23
00:01:20.734 --> 00:01:22.970
and it's why a pipeline is worth building.

24
00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:27.495
Group Policy analytics reads your exports
and tells you which settings have an

25
00:01:27.495 --> 00:01:29.347
equivalent, but only for Windows,

26
00:01:29.347 --> 00:01:32.295
and it only parses some
settings, and only in English.

27
00:01:32.295 --> 00:01:36.424
The Migrate button is called best
effort; Learn tells you not to move

28
00:01:36.424 --> 00:01:40.995
everything without review, and it simply
stops when two policies disagree.

29
00:01:40.995 --> 00:01:42.799
And everything in Graph here,

30
00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:47.008
both the analytics reports and the
settings catalog itself, is beta.

31
00:01:47.008 --> 00:01:49.345
Supported, but liable to change.

32
00:01:49.345 --> 00:01:51.120
Next, which cloud.

33
00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.915
Which cloud matters,
because Microsoft's import

34
00:01:57.915 --> 00:02:00.808
sample always calls the
worldwide Graph host.

35
00:02:00.808 --> 00:02:04.413
For a GCC tenant like this
estate's, that's correct:

36
00:02:04.413 --> 00:02:10.632
GCC uses the worldwide Graph and the same
Intune instance as commercial customers.

37
00:02:10.632 --> 00:02:14.217
GCC High and the Defense cloud
have their own Graph hosts,

38
00:02:14.217 --> 00:02:18.259
a shared government Intune instance
and their own admin center,

39
00:02:18.259 --> 00:02:21.157
and a token from one cloud
won't work in another.

40
00:02:21.157 --> 00:02:24.097
So the example maps each cloud name to its

41
00:02:24.097 --> 00:02:27.382
host and refuses any name
it doesn't recognize.

42
00:02:27.382 --> 00:02:29.490
Now, Microsoft's samples.

43
00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.440
Microsoft does give you a starting point.

44
00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.396
Learn's Intune migration guide
points to its Graph samples,

45
00:02:39.396 --> 00:02:42.787
and their settings catalog
folder has two scripts:

46
00:02:42.787 --> 00:02:47.227
one exports every policy to a file,
one imports a file as a new policy.

47
00:02:47.227 --> 00:02:49.744
Learn is candid that
they change your tenant,

48
00:02:49.744 --> 00:02:52.602
come as-is, and should be
tried somewhere safe first.

49
00:02:52.602 --> 00:02:56.737
The repository keeps an unmodified
copy, with hashes a test checks,

50
00:02:56.737 --> 00:03:00.270
and a harness that runs both
scripts against a fake Graph,

51
00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:03.953
replacing only the commands
they call outside themselves.

52
00:03:03.953 --> 00:03:08.430
Notice what's absent: no sample
reads the analytics through Graph.

53
00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:16.248
Running the samples against the
fake Graph shows what they do well,

54
00:03:16.248 --> 00:03:20.093
and the example keeps that: the
same create call, the same filter,

55
00:03:20.093 --> 00:03:23.643
the same paging, and policies
created with no assignments.

56
00:03:23.643 --> 00:03:25.255
The gaps are about safety.

57
00:03:25.255 --> 00:03:28.092
The import always calls
the worldwide host.

58
00:03:28.092 --> 00:03:30.918
It writes with whatever session
you happen to have open.

59
00:03:30.918 --> 00:03:32.293
There's no dry run.

60
00:03:32.293 --> 00:03:35.904
And if you run it twice, you get
two policies with the same name,

61
00:03:35.904 --> 00:03:38.431
because it never looks
at the tenant first.

62
00:03:38.431 --> 00:03:40.955
Each of those gaps is pinned by a test.

63
00:03:40.955 --> 00:03:43.440
There are more, on the record side.

64
00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.378
The rest of the gaps are about the record.

65
00:03:49.378 --> 00:03:53.968
The import quietly edits the file
before sending it, so what reached the

66
00:03:53.968 --> 00:03:58.715
tenant isn't what anyone reviewed; the
example refuses such a file instead.

67
00:03:58.715 --> 00:04:01.603
The import leaves nothing
for the security officer.

68
00:04:01.603 --> 00:04:06.609
The export stamps each file with the time,
so Git can never show a change, and it

69
00:04:06.609 --> 00:04:07.959
drops the scope tags,

70
00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:12.091
so a re-imported policy is visible
to the wrong administrators.

71
00:04:12.091 --> 00:04:14.615
And neither script knows
who owns a setting.

72
00:04:14.615 --> 00:04:17.872
The verdict: keep Microsoft's
create call as the model,

73
00:04:17.872 --> 00:04:21.710
and let the sync ask what's
already there before it writes.

74
00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.310
The read side has two parts.

75
00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:31.444
First, a gate from the validation
kit, which works per policy object:

76
00:04:31.444 --> 00:04:35.053
anything targeted in the
directory that isn't fully ready,

77
00:04:35.053 --> 00:04:38.360
or anything in scope that
was never imported, fails it.

78
00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.422
Then the move plan works per setting.

79
00:04:40.422 --> 00:04:44.258
Every setting gets a row and a
disposition: a candidate to move,

80
00:04:44.258 --> 00:04:49.147
a conflict between two policies, something
that belongs in Endpoint security,

81
00:04:49.147 --> 00:04:53.510
something with no catalog equivalent,
or something that stays behind.

82
00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:56.610
And here's the important part:
the plan decides nothing.

83
00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:59.450
A person decides, and records why.

84
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:08.663
On the write side, each policy is one
file in Git, and the file has two halves.

85
00:05:08.663 --> 00:05:12.222
The policy half is exactly
the body Graph will receive,

86
00:05:12.222 --> 00:05:16.176
in the shape Learn documents, so
what's reviewed is what's sent.

87
00:05:16.176 --> 00:05:21.131
The other half is the record: who approves
a change, which tool owns these settings,

88
00:05:21.131 --> 00:05:26.087
which old policies they came from, and a
decision record for every single setting.

89
00:05:26.087 --> 00:05:30.738
The setting identifiers in the two sample
files come from Learn's own examples,

90
00:05:30.738 --> 00:05:33.600
and the chapter's advice
is to export one from the

91
00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.030
admin center and compare
before trusting any other.

92
00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:43.580
The linter is where the
program's own rules live.

93
00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:45.705
Four rules carry most of the weight.

94
00:05:45.705 --> 00:05:48.743
Only settings Intune
owns go in the library.

95
00:05:48.743 --> 00:05:51.042
No setting may appear in two policies,

96
00:05:51.042 --> 00:05:54.455
which is exactly the conflict
a peer reviewer hunts for.

97
00:05:54.455 --> 00:05:56.692
Every setting needs a decision record.

98
00:05:56.692 --> 00:06:00.861
And anything that isn't a clean
candidate from the plan raises a warning,

99
00:06:00.861 --> 00:06:04.393
so a setting added by hand is a
choice someone made on purpose.

100
00:06:04.393 --> 00:06:08.111
One more rule watches for secrets,
because Learn says anyone

101
00:06:08.111 --> 00:06:11.606
who can read device configuration
can read those values.

102
00:06:11.606 --> 00:06:13.190
Then comes the diff.

103
00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:20.215
Now the heart of the sync:
it diffs before it writes.

104
00:06:20.215 --> 00:06:24.454
It reads the tenant's policies,
normalizes both sides so harmless

105
00:06:24.454 --> 00:06:28.928
formatting differences don't count,
and gives each policy one action.

106
00:06:28.928 --> 00:06:30.277
New ones get created.

107
00:06:30.277 --> 00:06:32.027
Matching ones are left alone.

108
00:06:32.027 --> 00:06:36.689
Anything that has drifted, or appears
twice, stops the whole release.

109
00:06:36.689 --> 00:06:39.803
By default it's a dry run
with read-only permission;

110
00:06:39.803 --> 00:06:42.765
only with the apply switch
does it ask for write.

111
00:06:42.765 --> 00:06:46.927
And it never updates or deletes,
even though Graph allows both.

112
00:06:46.927 --> 00:06:50.361
A policy that differs from Git
is a question for a person,

113
00:06:50.361 --> 00:06:52.450
not something to overwrite.

114
00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:59.675
Here are the facts the code
depends on, as Learn states them.

115
00:06:59.675 --> 00:07:03.002
Every call is beta, every
read needs only the device

116
00:07:03.002 --> 00:07:07.200
configuration read permission,
and only the create needs write.

117
00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:11.638
Learn lists all of them for the
worldwide and both US Government levels.

118
00:07:11.638 --> 00:07:14.888
The chapter is just as careful
about what Learn doesn't say.

119
00:07:14.888 --> 00:07:17.867
There's no Graph equivalent
of the Migrate button,

120
00:07:17.867 --> 00:07:20.338
so the second lab
compares the two by hand.

121
00:07:20.338 --> 00:07:24.765
And no Learn page confirms the
analytics import itself works in GCC

122
00:07:24.765 --> 00:07:29.710
High or the Defense cloud, so that
gets confirmed in a test tenant first.

123
00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:34.510
Who's involved?

124
00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:39.907
The developers and Intune engineers who
build the library, the reviewers who approve

125
00:07:39.907 --> 00:07:44.848
each file, and the owner named in every
file, who approves changes to that policy.

126
00:07:44.848 --> 00:07:48.473
The roles follow the same
least-privilege line as the code.

127
00:07:48.473 --> 00:07:51.953
Importing a policy object
for analysis needs an Intune

128
00:07:51.953 --> 00:07:55.435
administrator or a role with
two specific permissions.

129
00:07:55.435 --> 00:07:59.710
Reading, planning and the dry run
need only a read-only operator.

130
00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:02.520
Only the final apply needs
the built-in role that

131
00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.180
manages configuration profiles,
with write permission.

132
00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:12.730
Lab 1 runs on a laptop with no tenant.

133
00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:16.743
First you run the validation kit's
gate against its failing fixture,

134
00:08:16.743 --> 00:08:19.180
and see three real findings and a warning.

135
00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:23.038
Then comes the revealing step:
you run Microsoft's own import

136
00:08:23.038 --> 00:08:27.205
sample twice against the fake Graph,
and the policy appears twice.

137
00:08:27.205 --> 00:08:32.017
The call log shows two creates and not
a single read; the script never looked.

138
00:08:32.017 --> 00:08:36.940
Then you build the move plan, and the
eleven rows show every kind of disposition,

139
00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:40.955
including a hibernate setting
where two old policies disagree.

140
00:08:40.955 --> 00:08:43.560
Next, the lint and the sync.

141
00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:50.855
Then you lint the library against
the plan, and the only thing it

142
00:08:50.855 --> 00:08:54.810
reports is that hibernate conflict,
with the decision that settled it.

143
00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:59.165
The dry run says one policy will be
created and one is already in place,

144
00:08:59.165 --> 00:09:02.985
and applying it creates exactly
that one and writes a record.

145
00:09:02.985 --> 00:09:05.867
Point it at another cloud
and the host changes;

146
00:09:05.867 --> 00:09:08.522
give it a name it doesn't
know and it refuses.

147
00:09:08.522 --> 00:09:12.068
Then break something on purpose:
change one value in a file,

148
00:09:12.068 --> 00:09:15.910
and the sync reports drift and
refuses to write anything at all.

149
00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:18.710
Run the tests, and forty-six pass.

150
00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:27.135
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant with
Intune licenses, never production.

151
00:09:27.135 --> 00:09:30.343
You import a test policy
object into analytics,

152
00:09:30.343 --> 00:09:34.785
then run the gate and build the plan
for real, with read-only access.

153
00:09:34.785 --> 00:09:38.560
Any setting in your library that
isn't in the plan is a finding.

154
00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:41.289
The dry run asks only for read; the apply,

155
00:09:41.289 --> 00:09:46.235
under the profile manager role,
creates policies with no assignments.

156
00:09:46.235 --> 00:09:48.162
Then the interesting comparison:

157
00:09:48.162 --> 00:09:51.810
run Microsoft's Migrate button on
the same settings, and compare.

158
00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:57.222
Drill drift by editing a value by hand,
and finally delete everything the lab made.

159
00:09:57.222 --> 00:09:59.650
The sync itself never deletes.

160
00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:08.437
Each of these dangers has a guard, and most
of them come back to the same few ideas.

161
00:10:08.437 --> 00:10:11.247
One setting, one policy, one owning tool,

162
00:10:11.247 --> 00:10:16.076
checked in the pull request, so two
systems never fight over a value.

163
00:10:16.076 --> 00:10:19.238
Read before you write, so
running twice is harmless.

164
00:10:19.238 --> 00:10:22.750
Treat drift as a stop, never
something to overwrite.

165
00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:25.325
Keep the write permission
for the moment of writing.

166
00:10:25.325 --> 00:10:27.240
And because the APIs are beta,

167
00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:31.788
keep the pinned tests and run the
test-tenant lab before each release.

168
00:10:31.788 --> 00:10:34.049
When the policies are assigned later,

169
00:10:34.049 --> 00:10:38.350
Learn prefers filters over dynamic
groups that only Intune uses.

170
00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:43.450
Here's what done looks like.

171
00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:45.838
The gate passes for the whole population.

172
00:10:45.838 --> 00:10:50.551
The move plan travels with the pull
request, and every conflict has a decision.

173
00:10:50.551 --> 00:10:52.708
Each library file names its owner,

174
00:10:52.708 --> 00:10:57.563
its source policies and a decision per
setting, and no setting appears twice.

175
00:10:57.563 --> 00:11:01.462
The dry run is clean and someone
actually read what it will create.

176
00:11:01.462 --> 00:11:06.151
The release wrote its record, and afterward
the dry run shows nothing left to do.

177
00:11:06.151 --> 00:11:08.160
Everything was created unassigned,

178
00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:11.175
and the pinned samples are
still the ones you reviewed.

179
00:11:11.175 --> 00:11:15.263
Next, Chapter 7 builds machine
configuration packages.

180
00:11:15.263 --> 00:11:16.420
Thanks for watching.
